age 1.\n\nConsider $y'=\\dfrac{2x+3y}{x-4y}$, with $y(x_0)=y_0$." :multistep/id #uuid "08810770-5645-5bc0-96e4-f1697164f9fc" :multistep/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484496 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484430}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484586 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484439}}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484440 :multistep/context "Source question: Exercise **2.1.10**, [printed page 91 (PDF page 103)](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf#page=103).\n\nConsider\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\quad B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\quad C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\quad D=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\quad E=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind each expression if possible; explain why if it is not possible.\n\nPart (h) continues on printed page 92 (PDF page 104)." :multistep/id #uuid "18a019ce-b91b-55bf-b2be-d55bb374c90f" :multistep/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484483 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484607}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484488 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484500}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484494 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484515}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484509 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484526}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484514 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484576}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484540 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484617}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484578 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484462}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484601 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484481}}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484499 :multistep/context "Source question: Problem 3(a–b), PDF page 4.\n\nLet $C:\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle x(t),y(t)\\rangle$, $a\\le t\\le b$, and let $\\mathbf F=\\langle f,g\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf G=\\langle-g,f\\rangle$." :mu